<section xml:id="algorithms_sorting">
  <title>Sorting</title>
  <p>Another common operation computers need to do with data is to sort. <em>What are the top 10 results on a web search
    for the term <q>sort algorithm</q>?</em>, <em>Rank the states of the US based on population</em>, <em>Print an
    alphabetical class roster</em> - these are all examples of tasks that require putting data in some kind of order.
    Or, perhaps we want to use an algorithm like binary search that will only work on ordered data.</p>
  <p>Much like how we can use multiple different strategies for searching, there are many possible algorithms we can use
    to sort a list of data. Each algorithm uses a different strategy to rearrange the data - these different strategies
    mean the algorithms behave differently. Some algorithms will be faster than others, maybe all the time, maybe just
    in certain situations. Some algorithms will produce useful results if we stop them early - like finding the smallest
    5 things without completely sorting the list.</p>
  <p>In the next few sections will learn a few sort algorithms so we can compare various strategies.</p>
  <exercise label="algorithms_sorting-ex1">
    <statement>
      <p>Why might a program sort a list before searching it?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Sorting makes binary search possible.</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Sorting guarantees every search takes one comparison.</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Sorting removes duplicate values automatically.</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Sorting changes every value into an index.</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
  <exercise label="algorithms_sorting-ex2">
    <statement>
      <p>Which tasks are examples of sorting data? Select all that apply.</p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Producing an alphabetical class roster</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Ranking states by population</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Checking whether a password is correct</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Adding two numbers</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Loading a file from disk</p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
</section>
